  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThe main forecast concern this period will be the return of the heat \nand above normal temperatures to the area through the weekend into \nnext week. This heat will come courtesy of a upper level ridge that \ncurrently resides over the Four Corners region. This feature will \nbegin to slide eastward and build over the the western Plains today. \nThis will provide local height rises allowing highs to warm a few \ndegrees despite surface high pressure in place. Expect highs in the \nmid 80 to near 90. By Saturday, the local area comes further under \nthe purview of the ridge as the axis moves into the central Plains. \nIn addition, strong WAA will get underway at the surface. This will \nallow highs to rise into the upper 80s to mid 90s. Sunday, the ridge \naxis moves directly over the local area. This coupled with continued \nstrong WAA will drive highs into the mid to upper 90s. \n\nThe local area then remains under the influence of the upper level \nridge through the duration of next week as it remains in control \nfrom the southeastern CONUS back into the central and southern \nPlains. This will keep highs in the mid to upper 90s. While \ntemperatures will be 10-15 degrees above normal, dewpoints in the \nlow to mid 60s through Tuesday will keep heat index values Sunday \nthrough Tuesday in the 95-102 degree range. By Wednesday and \nThursday, dewpoints begin to rise a bit into the upper 60s to near \n70, yielding some heat index values approaching 105. It is this \nperiod that may need to be monitored for heat products.\n\nOtherwise, conditions look to remain mostly dry the next week. The \none exception will be for areas of northern Missouri where slight \nchance (15-25%) PoPs will be possible nightly from Sunday night \nthrough Wednesday night. This will come as models suggest periodic, \nweak upper level disturbances will try to push into the upper ridge. \nThe recent model runs have trended drier so confidence is low in \nprecipitation chances.",
